    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The efficacy hinges on the ionizable lipids' protonation state, which governs complex assembly under acidic endosomal conditions. As endosomes mature, the decreasing $\\text{pH}$ protonates the amine moieties within the lipid headgroups, inducing a critical change in physicochemical properties. This protonation facilitates electrostatic interaction and subsequent destabilization of the endosomal membrane via a \"proton sponge\" effect, leading to membrane permeabilization. This targeted disruption allows for the efficient translocation of the nucleic acid cargo across the endosomal/cytosolic boundary, dramatically boosting downstream translational output.",
      "source": "target",
